Henry V
By William Shakespeare

### **ACT IV, SCENE I — The English Camp at Agincourt, Night**

**[Enter KING HENRY and GLOUCESTER]**

**KING HENRY:**
Gloucester, it’s true—we’re in great danger.
All the more reason our courage should be greater.

**[Enter BEDFORD]**

Good morning, brother Bedford. — Gracious heaven!
There’s some goodness even in bad things,
if people would only look for it.
Our troublesome neighbor (the French) keeps us alert and early rising,
which is healthy and productive.
So, we can draw honey even from weeds—
and find a moral lesson in the devil himself.

**[Enter ERPINGHAM]**

Good morning, old Sir Thomas Erpingham.
A soft pillow would suit that white head of yours
better than a hard French field.

**ERPINGHAM:**
Not so, my liege. I like this bed better,
for now I can say, “Here I lie like a king.”

**KING HENRY:**
Lend me your cloak, Sir Thomas. — Brothers,
send my greetings to the other princes in camp.
Wish them good morning from me,
and ask them to come to my tent soon.

**GLOUCESTER:**
We will, my liege.
**[Gloucester and Bedford exit]**

**ERPINGHAM:**
Shall I stay with you, Your Grace?

**KING HENRY:**
No, good knight. Go with my brothers
and the rest of the lords.
I need a moment alone
to talk with my own thoughts.

**ERPINGHAM:**
Heaven bless you, noble Harry!
**[Exits]**

**KING HENRY:**
Thank you, old heart! You speak cheerfully.

**[Enter PISTOL]**

**PISTOL:**
Who goes there?

**KING HENRY:**
A friend.

**PISTOL:**
Tell me, are you an officer,
or just a common soldier?

**KING HENRY:**
I’m a gentleman serving in a company.

**PISTOL:**
Do you carry a pike?

**KING HENRY:**
I do. What about you?

**PISTOL:**
I’m as good a gentleman as the emperor.

**KING HENRY:**
Then you’re better than the king.

**PISTOL:**
The king’s a fine fellow—a heart of gold!
Full of life and fame,
born of good parents and strong of fist.
I’d kiss his dirty shoe,
and from my heart I love that noble man.
What’s your name?

**KING HENRY:**
Harry *le Roi.*

**PISTOL:**
*Le Roi?* That’s Cornish! You from Cornwall?

**KING HENRY:**
No, I’m Welsh.

**PISTOL:**

**KING HENRY:**
Yes.

**PISTOL:**
Tell him I’ll bash his head with his own leek
on Saint David’s Day.

**KING HENRY:**
Then don’t wear your dagger in your cap that day,
or he might knock that instead.

**PISTOL:**
Are you his friend?

**KING HENRY:**
And his relative too.

**PISTOL:**
Then the fig to you!

**KING HENRY:**
Thanks. Heaven be with you.

**PISTOL:**
My name’s Pistol.
**[Exits]**

**KING HENRY:**
That name suits his fiery temper.

**[Enter FLUELLEN and GOWER]**

**GOWER:**

**FLUELLEN:**
Quiet, man! Speak softly!
It’s a terrible thing when the ancient rules of war
aren’t kept. Look at Pompey the Great—
you’ll find no gossip or idle chatter in his camp.

**GOWER:**
But the enemy’s been noisy all night.

**FLUELLEN:**
If the enemy’s a fool and a braggart,
should we be fools and braggarts too?
Use your conscience, man.

**GOWER:**
I’ll speak more quietly.

**FLUELLEN:**
Please do. I beg you.
**[They exit]**

**KING HENRY:**
Though he seems old-fashioned,
there’s great care and courage in that Welshman.

**[Enter BATES and WILLIAMS]**

**WILLIAMS:**
Brother Bates, isn’t that the morning breaking over there?

**BATES:**
I think so. But we’ve little reason to wish for daylight.

**WILLIAMS:**
We may see the dawn,
but I doubt we’ll see its end. — Who goes there?

**KING HENRY:**
A friend.
**[He approaches]**

**WILLIAMS:**
Under whose command do you serve?

**KING HENRY:**
Under Sir Thomas Erpingham.

**WILLIAMS:**
A good old commander, and kind man.
What does he think of our situation?

**KING HENRY:**
He thinks we’re like sailors stranded on a sandbar,
waiting to be washed away by the next tide.

**BATES:**
Has he said that to the king?

**KING HENRY:**
No, and it wouldn’t be right to.
For though I’ll tell you this —
I think the king is only a man like me.
The violet smells the same to him as to me;
the air feels the same;
his senses are just human.
So when he faces fear,
he feels it as we do.
But he must never show it —
for if he did, it would weaken the army.

**BATES:**
He may look brave,
but I’ll bet, on this cold night,
he’d rather be up to his neck in the Thames.
And so would I, if it meant we could go home.

**KING HENRY:**
Honestly, I think the king would not wish himself anywhere else.

**BATES:**
Then I wish he were here alone!
At least he’d be ransomed,
and many poor souls would be saved.

**KING HENRY:**
I think you don’t hate him,
you just want to see how others feel.
For my part, I could die happily in his company,
knowing his cause is just and his quarrel honorable.

**WILLIAMS:**
That’s more than we know.

**BATES:**
Or should ask.
It’s enough to know we’re his subjects.
If his cause is wrong, our obedience wipes away our guilt.

**WILLIAMS:**
But if the cause is wrong,
the king has a heavy reckoning to make.
All those dead soldiers—arms, legs, and heads—
will rise on Judgment Day and cry,
“We died there!”
Some swearing, some calling for surgeons,
some thinking of wives and children left behind.
Few die well in battle, I think.
If they die unprepared, the fault lies with the king who led them.

**KING HENRY:**
If a merchant’s son dies sinfully at sea,
is the father to blame for sending him?
Of course not.
The king is not responsible for how each man dies,
any more than a father for his son’s fate.
Each man owes duty to his king,
but his soul is his own.
So every soldier should cleanse his conscience,
like a sick man preparing for death.
If he dies, he gains heaven;
if he lives, the preparation was still worth it.

**WILLIAMS:**
True — whoever dies in sin, it’s on his own head,
not the king’s.

**BATES:**
I don’t want the king to answer for me,
but I’ll fight bravely for him.

**KING HENRY:**
I heard the king swear he would never be ransomed.

**WILLIAMS:**
He said that to keep our spirits up.
When we’re dead, he can be ransomed for all we know.

**KING HENRY:**
If that happens, I’ll never trust his word again.

**WILLIAMS:**
That’s an empty threat!
You might as well try to freeze the sun with a feather fan.
It’s foolish to say such a thing.

**KING HENRY:**
You speak rather boldly!
I’d be angry if the time were right.

**WILLIAMS:**
Then let’s make it a quarrel for later—if you live.

**KING HENRY:**
Agreed.

**WILLIAMS:**
How will I know you again?

**KING HENRY:**
Give me something of yours, and I’ll wear it in my hat.
If you see it later, you can challenge me.

**WILLIAMS:**
Here’s my glove. Give me one of yours.

**KING HENRY:**
There. Take it.

**WILLIAMS:**
I’ll wear this in my cap.
If you come to me after tomorrow and say,
“This is my glove,”
I’ll hit you across the face.

**KING HENRY:**
If I live, I’ll hold you to that.

**WILLIAMS:**
You wouldn’t dare.

**KING HENRY:**
I would, even if you stood beside the king himself.

**WILLIAMS:**
Keep your word, then. Farewell.

**BATES:**
Come, be friends, you English fools.
We’ve got enough quarrels with the French as it is.
**[They exit]**

**KING HENRY:**
Everything on the king!
Our lives, our souls, our sins—
all laid on the king!
What a hard fate to be born great,
to bear every fool’s breath and blame.
Private men have peace of heart—
but kings, only ceremony.
What are you, ceremony?
Nothing but rank and form,
that makes others fear you,
but leaves you less happy than they.
You drink not true respect,
but poisoned flattery.
Be sick, great greatness—
can ceremony cure you?
Can it command health
like it commands a beggar’s knee?
No, you proud illusion—
I see through you.
It’s not the crown, or sceptre, or sword,
or throne, or glory of the world—
none of these can bring a king
the sleep of a poor man,
who, with a full belly and an empty mind,
lies down to rest after a hard day’s work.
But for ceremony,
such a man would live better than a king.

**[Enter ERPINGHAM]**

**ERPINGHAM:**
My lord, your nobles are worried by your absence.
They’re searching the camp for you.

**KING HENRY:**
Good old knight, gather them all at my tent.
I’ll be there soon.
**[Hands him back the cloak]**

**ERPINGHAM:**
I’ll do it, my lord.
**[Exits]**

**KING HENRY:**
O God of battles!
Strengthen my soldiers’ hearts.
Take away their fear,
and blind them to the enemy’s numbers,
lest they lose courage.
Not today, O Lord! Not today,
think not on my father’s sin
in seizing the crown!
I’ve reburied Richard’s body,
and wept more tears of repentance
than drops of blood were shed.
I pay five hundred poor men each year
to pray for mercy on that blood.
I’ll do even more—

**[A trumpet sounds]**

The day has come.
My friends and all things wait for me.
**[Exits]**


